description:
In smime-tree/src/encrypt.rs, the function ecdh_wrap_cek() derives a key-encryption key (KEK) into a plain Vec<u8>:

    let mut kek = vec![0u8; kek_len];
    ansi_x963_kdf::derive_key_into::<D>(shared_secret_bytes, &shared_info_der, &mut kek)?;
    // ... used to wrap CEK ...
    // kek dropped here with no zeroization

The KEK is derived from the ECDH shared secret and is used once to wrap the CEK.  After use it should be scrubbed from memory.  Instead it is dropped as a plain Vec<u8>, leaving key material on the heap until the allocator reclaims that region.

Previous work (review pass 6) correctly zeroized the CEK via Zeroizing<Vec<u8>> in encrypt_aes_cbc().  The KEK in ecdh_wrap_cek() was not addressed in that pass and still leaks.

Fix: wrap kek in Zeroizing<Vec<u8>> at the point of allocation:

    let mut kek = Zeroizing::new(vec![0u8; kek_len]);

The Zeroizing newtype implements DerefMut so all existing uses of kek (as &mut [u8] for the KDF, and as &[u8; N] slices for AES-KW) continue to work without further changes.  Zeroize is already a dependency of smime-tree (used by encrypt_aes_cbc).

Acceptance criteria:
- kek is declared as Zeroizing<Vec<u8>> in ecdh_wrap_cek
- cargo test -p smime-tree passes
- cargo clippy -p smime-tree -- -D warnings passes
